  "title": "The role of every torus atom",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "Proved by hand: a torus atom (a torus knot space less a point P) grown at a line of any orientable knot space with a Kasteleyn law allows on its three outer points the replaced line's six patterns when its torus is unsatisfiable in the class read on the atom and free at P (each of P's sides flipped leaves a satisfiable law), and all eight when the torus is satisfiable and free; in an unsatisfiable class it holds a unit in its region in every state satisfying its lines. Hence the atom rule (one acting class per atom; all acting leaves the host's satisfiability; each acting atom holds a unit) for any such atoms on any host, adjacent or not. T1 computed on 29 lattice-torus types: the 8 class-forced ones act in exactly their torus's unsatisfiable class on all 60 splices each (K_2-K_4), the 21 all-satisfiable ones are transparent.",
